export abstract anchor FactoryOrchestration:
    orchestration:
        The factory runs independently and owns general orchestration. Agents
        perform explicit execution steps of tasks; no agent owns the general
        orchestration loop.
    integrationPrinciple:
        Deterministic controls with agent-based wiring is a general factory
        design principle. Code implements the controls and their routine
        execution semantics. Agents establish the environment-specific wiring
        for harness connections, build and test commands, remote machines, and
        delivery integrations. Apply this principle beyond remote builds.
        Wiring centers on known factory buttons, meaning defined operations
        with deterministic controls. Those operations must be configurable
        enough for agents to write integration code connecting them to the
        environment. Agents save reusable wiring; ordinary code executes it
        afterward, bringing agents back when wiring needs changes or repair.
        The operation set is defined in FactoryOperations.
    wiringRepresentation:
        Saved wiring uses settings for simple connections and code modules for
        complex ones. Settings record the command or address, environment,
        credential reference, and timeout the operation needs. Code modules
        implement behavior that settings cannot express. Both forms sit behind
        the same operation interface and must pass that operation's standard
        check before use. Agents may write or repair either form within the
        existing configuration and authorization.
    wiringValidation:
        Each factory button defines a standard check that its agent-written
        wiring must pass before use. The check verifies the integration's
        expected behavior, such as running a small remote build and returning
        its result. Exact checks for each operation remain to be defined.
    factoryStateOperations:
        Agents add tasks, revise plans and dependencies, and report results
        through defined factory operations rather than directly editing
        internal factory records or its database. Ordinary code validates
        permissions and consistency and preserves recovery history for these
        changes. Agents may still write project files and integration wiring
        as assigned. The operation transport and storage implementation remain
        choices for the consuming project.
    operationRetries:
        Give factory operation requests identifiers so retries after a lost
        response return the original result without repeating the action.
        Prevent duplicate tasks, builds, or agent launches from retries of the
        same request. The identifier representation and retention mechanism
        remain implementation choices.
    wiringRepair:
        When saved wiring breaks, the factory automatically assigns an agent
        to restore its intended behavior within the existing configuration and
        authorization. Repaired wiring must pass the operation's standard
        check before use. Changes to the intended setup, such as selecting a
        different provider, buying capacity, or changing delivery destinations,
        require engineer input. Failed repairs follow the existing escalation
        path.
    executionHarnesses:
        The initial supported agent harnesses are Claude Code, Codex, and
        OpenCode. Harness integration must accommodate API-backed accounts and
        subscription accounts. Integration contracts remain unspecified.
        The engineer currently uses Claude, Codex, MiMo, and DeepSeek, with
        OpenCode in the setup. The exact mapping of each model or account to a
        harness has not been confirmed.
    engineerInput:
        When the smartest agent determines that a question is for the engineer,
        the factory obtains input from the engineer. While the answer is
        pending, independent work continues as much as possible. Only steps
        that depend on the answer are blocked by that question.
    monitoring:
        The engineer can monitor the factory through both a dashboard and
        updates through the conversational agent. The dashboard shows orders,
        steps, agents, and questions requiring engineer input. Update delivery
        mechanisms and frequency remain unspecified.
    engineerControl:
        The engineer initiates work with a prompt and can start or gracefully
        shut down the factory. Routine execution is autonomous; engineer controls
        are deliberately minimal and do not provide per-order pause, resume,
        cancellation, or revision controls for micromanagement. Engineer answers
        to questions escalated by agents remain supported.
